SECTION I.
Introduction

Augmented Reality (AR) is considered to be a new medium of creative expression that can enrich the manner in which human beings experience reality [1] , [2] . Applications such as SnapChat 1 and Pokmon GO 2 have led to an increase in awareness and everyday use of AR. The overlay of this digital information onto the users’ surroundings provides an opportunity to multiple industries and scenarios, including the enhancement of the tourism experience. The tourism industry is one of the main contributions to the gross domestic product in many countries and has a direct impact on the development of a country’s economy. Traditional city tours are frequently marred by time constraints, varying visitor interest levels and communication issues. Marketing a destination has become challenging and many researchers in the tourism industry argue that it has become more competitive [3] . Thus, AR can be the new modern technology which will up rise a destination through a mobile-based virtual experience which modifies current consumers’ prior, en route, and post-experience [4] . AR applications were found to create meaningful, enjoyable and enhanced tourism experiences [5] .

The hypothesis of this study is that a location driven AR treasure hunt game enriches a user’s experience in terms of learning and entertainment, during a heritage tour. Given this hypothesis three main questions were formulated:

    How does an AR enhanced city tour compare with a traditional walk-around?

    What UX features are required to elicit interest and what are the potential inhibitors intimidating visitors when using the AR application?

    How can AR be used to provide an improved experience of Maltese Heritage and will individuals learn from this process?

Our study starts with a review of literature, structured in relevant hyper-themes. Tourism AR projects were investigated to inform the methodology of this study and UX requirements for the development of two AR prototype applications. Given the nature of the study a justification for mixed-methods approach is provided. Following the on-site experiment, data collected from pre-post test interviews, application log data and survey were analyzed both separately and triangulated together. In the final section, a summary of conclusions, limitations and recommendations for further research are provided.
SECTION II.
Background
A. AR in Tourism

The use of modern technology is crucial for many destination-marketing organizations. Many museums and historical sites have already started to make use of media, such as movie clips and photography to enhance visitor experience [6] . However, this approach many times lacks the potential of modern interactive technology [6] . AR provides significant benefits for many industries due to its nature of mixed environment, or computer enhancement of real world setting [7] –​ [9] .

As [10] outlines, the proper use of AR for in the tourist heritage sector still requires investigation to enhance the tourist experience. However, if implemented well, AR has the potential of becoming a mainstream technological tool in the tourism industry in the future due to its indoor and outdoor capabilities which will enhance the tourist experience, learning and fun.
B. AR for Learning

Educators have recognized that Virtual Reality (VR) has hidden benefits that help students in education, namely history, science and mathematics [11] . Moreover, [12] , found that the feelings of presence that VR may induce can assist the learning process. Whilst VR is more concerned with immersion [12] , AR’s potential for learning can be exploited in museums, heritage areas, and tourist attractions, using a basic smartphone device. In line with Gardner’s theory of multiple intelligences, which suggests that "traditional psychometric views of intelligence are too limited and that all people have different kinds of intelligences" [13] , multimedia elements can aid learning via a multi-sensory approach. By embedding different media like images, video, text and 3D models within an AR application, different learning styles are catered for. For example, some people prefer visual learning over auditory learning [13] .

Due to its potential, as stated by [14] , "AR is changing the face of education and creating new opportunities for improvement in quality of teaching and learning and trying to reflect the user’s information needs". [15] ’s systematic literature review about AR found that AR-based systems were more effective in improving learners’ motivation and satisfaction than traditional ones, especially for situated, inquiry based, and self-regulated learning. However, the review found that improved applications still lack adeptness, with them being too much emphasizing on entertainment and generally being limited by simple visuals [15] . On the other hand, [16] , found that usability or ease-of-use appear to be the biggest challenge in some applications but then again, most studies concluded that AR enhances enjoyment, motivation and interaction for learners.
C. AR in Entertainment

In the entertainment sector, AR is mainly used for interactive games which combine the real world and virtual world together. With the aid of AR, the user can play with 3D characters imposed on real time backgrounds. In addition, with the use of mobile phones, some organizations introduced AR in their magazine which requires the reader to point the camera to a marker and animated characters appear on the mobile screen [17] . In this scenario AR is used to give life to text stories through further multi-sensory reader immersion. In 2016, the release of the ‘Pokémon GO’ AR game, took the world by a storm. The game quickly became a worldwide phenomenon, mainly by exploiting AR in a time of significant developments in mobile devices. This marked a sudden leap in mass awareness and popularity of AR. The scope of the game was to catch animated Pokémon appearing in the real-world, which were visible through the smartphone’s camera.
D. Related AR Projects

Several authors list multiple examples of using AR in tourism, namely, augmented walks, where tourists are placed within the real environment, but with the possibility to view additional artificial information in the form of 3D reconstructions of monuments, either via digital screens, or head-mounted displays [18] –​ [22] . In 2009, the region of Tuscany created the first AR smartphone application which was created to guide tourists around Tuscany by adding a layer of guidance, content and entertainment to physical locations seen through the smartphone’s camera view [23] .

The city of Vienna already provides a tourist guide application which guides users to different locations via navigation, as well as is able to provide location-based information on nearby places, which can be selected at will and share this information via social media [24] . Another example of a destination that aims to enhance the overall tourist experience through AR is Dublin. The initiative to utilize AR to strengthen and promote the city of Dublin as an ‘innovative city’ started in 2012 as a cooperation between the Manchester Metropolitan University, the Dublin Institute of Technology and the City Council of Dublin. [3] stated, "This application will provide a platform to superimpose tourism relevant information, reconstruct and revive stories of the past, assisting the tourist in creating an emotional experience of the intangible product". This application will not only be of benefit to tourists but also to citizens who want to learn about relevant information about the history of a ‘An Post Museum’ of the city. This application contains historical text, pictures and videos through AR which aid users to gain information about a site to create awareness and enhance the tourists’ experience in the context of Dublin’s historical heritage. For the users who had little or no idea of the concept of AR, a map highlighting AR-enabled contents in and around the ‘An Post Museum’. Moreover, because of the success of AR in the Museum, Dublin decided to also create an AR application to help with the outside city which increased steadily the tourists’ experience of learning about new cites [25] , strengthen and promote the city of Dublin, [26] .
E. UX requirements for Mobile/Handheld AR applications

According to [25] , identification of UX requirements for AR applications is limited. A reoccurring theme is simplicity and design of the user interface [18] –​ [21] . Good performance of the application with less reaction and loading times [18] –​ [21] . Therefore, the size of the application needs to be controlled to facilitate the installation process. Safety and security issues are a priority [18] –​ [21] . Application should be accessible at any time and available on different platforms [18] –​ [20] . Given the high probability of city tour time-constraints, the application needs to be efficient to attract utilization [18] –​ [21] . A search and browse button feature makes the AR application more user friendly to access context-aware relevant information [22] . Finally, proper use of good user interface designs are a must in an application [3] . These UX requirements should aim to increase the overall tourist’s experience in retrieving personalized multimedia information by means of a user-friendly, multi-language interface.
SECTION III.
Methodology
A. Research Methods

Given that this study needs to connect quantitative data with an interpretative investigation of human behaviour, a pragmatic mixed methods approach was adopted [27] . This approach has also been previously applied in tourism and AR sectors, where a quantitative approach was ideal to examine topics such as user requirements in a mobile computing context [3] and a qualitative approach, to examine the impact of cultural differences on behavioral intentions for a more accurate use of AR [28] .
