C. Survey Design

The survey questionnaire was divided in two sections. The first section gathered age/gender demographic data and presented generic questions about AR. The second section provided a demo of the game, after-which a series of questions where provided to capture the respondents views on the prototype. These questions seek to investigate whether AR functions improve the visitor’s experience.
D. Pre-Post Test Interviews and Data Logger

Six participants, who had experienced a traditional city walk-around, were invited for a pre-test interview, following which they made use of the AR applications. After the experience a post-test interview was conducted. The pre-test interviews provided control data with the post-test interviews supplying the experimental data. While roaming around Valletta to locate the markers, location readings and behavior data were recorded to a log file. This methodology was employed to triangulate interview transcripts with tracking data, thus enabling a cross-examination of results with multiple data sources.
SECTION IV.
Results and Discussion

Using a mixed method approach, survey, pre-post test interviews and data log files saved on user’s devices were analysed first separately and consequently triangulated. Congruent with our hypothesis, the main objective of this evaluation was to investigate if and how individuals prefer to use AR during a walk-around. In addition, the effectiveness of different UX designs in terms of the overall tour experience, learning and entertainment.
