All participants, with the exception of RP4 and RP5, never made use of AR. There was no noticeable benefit of having prior experience of AR as time taken by RP1-RP5 was very similar (UX2: 13-16 minutes). However, while participants navigating with the live map (UX2) took an average of 14 minutes to complete the treasure-hunt, the two participants using the static map (UX1) took an average of 25 minutes. These results confirmed the need of GPS navigation tools within the AR treasure-hunt game.
D. UX Design

There were mixed preferences regarding buttons positioning, with RP1, RP2 preferring bottom positioning and RP3 and RP4 at the side. All participants found the hints and icons throughout the game to be useful. In pre-test interviews UX1 users thought it would be more challenging and fun to locate the markers the traditional way, rather than using the live map and hints. Following the experience, while enjoying the extra challenge, participants suggested some hints would have been useful. Tracking data also confirmed the difficulty of participants navigating without hints. The two media which were deemed most appealing and useful for learning, were the 3D models and video elements.

With regards to UX improvements, RP1, being a young person, suggested adding a reward system using animations. The other five respondents appreciated the minimalistic and green complementary colours in the game. RP5, being the oldest person from the group at age 65+ suggested to have bigger buttons for easier and quicker access, also adjust the font size according to the liking of the user and also include a voice-over the text.

Adding different languages for a better experience was another remark, as also suggested by [19] and [20] . Following the experience, RP3 recommended extending the use of AR to museum tours.
E. Analysis with other studies

[10] , argued that AR is of particular interest to the tourism industry, in the way it is capable to reveal information on the surroundings by putting relevant data into a virtual space. Moreover, [29] concluded that this phenomenon is interesting to experiment with in heritage sites. In our survey, we found that an overwhelming number of respondents agree to the use-fullness of AR to discover a heritage site.

[24] concludes that the majority of the users appreciate the flexibility provided in the system build, i.e. the ability to use the system as a tour guide, a map or a guidebook. This finding was also confirmed in our survey, with participants finding AR helpful to learn. [28] assert that AR promotes a good atmosphere at a new destination. Similarly, in our study AR was viewed to provide a more enjoyable tour experience.

The live map feature was much appreciated by participants. This result is corroborated in [24] ’s study, where all respondents expressed their positive opinion on the location-aware navigation. Finally, [14] , stated that UX is crucial for an application to allow users to learn about heritage. UX was an important factor which was noticed by the respondents with positive feedback during the interview conducted.
SECTION V.
Conclusion

Our study is in agreement with existing research that AR has a great potential for tourism. Location driven UI and game elements are two main themes that emerged from our study. Through a mixed-methods approach, we can confidently conclude that AR does indeed help users to learn in a fun and interactive way. Whilst the initial hypothesis has been accepted, this study also reveals that AR in tourism applications is greatly enhanced through:

    The inclusion of location-based features and Live-maps.

    A UX Design which is easy to learn and use during a fast city tour.

    Gamification.

    Knowledge rich media to provide insights about a place and assist learning.

The inclusion of marker-less AR technology is also suggested. [10] argues that technology needs to be constantly implemented in tourism destinations to stay competitive and attractive for tourists. Although, we managed to create a solution to enhance the tourist’s experience using basic equipment and popular tools, a challenge lies in scaling the solution to cover wider areas. Issues like cost-effectiveness, time and equipment can be addressed with the creation of a framework that enables developers to produce content-rich AR related to tourism.

